.TH LN 1 "Riplix" "Ripple"
.SH NAME
ln \- create hard or symbolic links
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
ln [-fsn] target linkname
ln [-fsn] target ... directory
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Without -s, create another directory entry for the same inode. Hard links
require the same filesystem and cannot link a directory.
.PP
With -s, store target as a symbolic link. The target need not exist. Relative
targets are interpreted from the directory containing the link, not the
working directory in which ln was run. A symbolic link can name a directory
or cross a guest mount point.
.PP
Existing destination entries are preserved unless -f is given. -n explicitly
selects this default. If the last operand is a directory, create links there
using the basenames of the target operands.
.PP
ls -l displays symbolic link targets. Removing or renaming a link changes the
link itself. Plain cp follows source links; cp -P and recursive cp preserve
links unless -L (follow all) or -H (follow command-line links) is selected.
.PP
Targets hold at most 511 native characters, and pathname resolution follows
at most 40 symbolic links before returning ELOOP. Fuse-X hides host symlinks
under /mnt/host and rejects creating symbolic links in that mount.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
ln -s rift /bin/vi
ln -s /usr/man /manuals
ln -sf new-target old-link
.fi
